PAGE SHAPE (honesty): the web page renders NO API data of its own (manifest: 0 data sources,
2 panels, 1 state=success, 11 strings, requiredCount=14 — "renders from navigation args /
local state"). It edits an in-memory query string against a STATIC install-wide curated
schema catalog and surfaces a deterministic Run help message (there is NO browser SQL-
execution endpoint). The Android port is faithful to that shape: a SqlPlaygroundPageViewModel
hoists the editor string + Run reduction into a StateFlow<SqlPlaygroundUiState> (the native
analogue of the web localStorage 'ai.sqlPlayground.draft' persistence, ADR-002), and the
stateless SqlPlaygroundPageContent draws the two GlassPanels from that state. All derivation
(runOutcomeFor / canRunSql / by-name catalog sort) lives in the framework-free
SqlPlaygroundPageModel.kt; the composable only resolves i18n + draws.

CURATED CATALOG (honesty, no scope narrowing): the web `CURATED_CATALOG` const (5 tables:
drives, charging_sessions, vehicles, alerts, signal_log_view; 33 columns total) is ported
VERBATIM into SqlPlaygroundCatalog. These are language-neutral schema identifiers, SQL types,
and technical descriptions the web component itself HARDCODES (not i18n keys), stored SI
(distance_m, duration_s, *_wh, *_mps, *_w) per the Phase-42 SI-on-disk contract — so they are
mirrored as a static Kotlin structure, not routed through strings.xml, exactly matching the
web's own implementation. The by-name ordering matches the web `sortedTables` memo.

AI-DRAFTER BOUNDARY (honesty, no silent drift — Covenant #9): the web page also mounts the
optional <AINLSqlPlayground onApply=…/> AI drafter via `withAiFeature('nl-sql-playground')`,
which is ABSENT in AI-off mode (the default) and propose-only when on. That AI drafter is a
SEPARATE, feature-gated shared-surface parity unit (its own prompt
p3-android/shared-meaningful/A-0035-AINLSqlPlayground, its own native surface
io.teslasync.android.sharedsurfaces.ainlsqlplayground.AINLSqlPlayground + ViewModel/Source/
Model + unit tests already on this branch) and is NOT among THIS page's 14 required parity
items (the prompt's PARITY block lists exactly: GlassPanel1, GlassPanel2, success state, 11
strings). The AINLSqlPlaygroundSource binds an AI-mode gate + an AI SSE draft stream that the
app DI graph (DataContainer) does NOT yet expose, and wiring it would require editing
DataContainer.kt — OUTSIDE this prompt's allowed-files set (poweruser/**, NavHost
registration, res/values*/strings.xml, this log). No page on the branch currently embeds it.
Per Covenant #4 (no stubs as final) it is NOT embedded with a hardcoded `aiEnabled=false`
fake gate (that would be a stub, not the real propose-only contract). The manual-editor +
curated-catalog baseline (web ADR-015 "I3 baseline intact: renders regardless of the AI
toggle") is implemented IN FULL; the AI section is left to its own shared-surface wiring
phase. This boundary is documented here, not hidden.

NAV NOTE: the `powerSql` -> /power/sql destination already exists in Destinations.kt L156
(pre-existing metadata row; RouteTableTest asserts "/power/sql" to "powerSql"); this prompt
only attaches host content via PageHosts.register("powerSql"), and the deep link is auto-
registered by TeslaSyncNavHost. Registration is wired in TeslaSyncApplication.onCreate
alongside the sibling A7 page hosts (idempotent).

GATE PATH NOTE: the project Gradle path is ':android' (settings.gradle.kts maps ':android' ->
app/); the prompt snippet's ':app:' is the same single app module. Tasks run were
:android:assembleDebug / :android:lintDebug / :android:testDebugUnitTest.

STRINGS-CATALOG NOTE (honesty): res/values/strings.xml is the generated i18n catalog, but the
prompt's allowed-files set explicitly includes res/values*/strings.xml, and the 11 powerSql.*
keys did not previously exist in it (the web page declares them via inline `t(key, default)`
